The good news is that the root cause is exactly the same, and fixing the backend will instantly fix your Status screen.
Right now, the Status screen's workflow history relies purely on what the backend saves into the VOUCHER_WORKFLOW history table. Because the backend is currently hardcoding null for the scheduled time during the async batch creation, your Status screen's UI says, *"I see a submission, but no time is attached, so I will render 'Immediate Execution'."*
By applying this single backend fix, you will satisfy the requirement to maintain both times: the Creator's time will permanently lock into Step 1 of the history, and the Final Approver's time (if overridden) will lock into the final step.
Here is the exact fix for the backend that will make the Status screen display correctly.
### The Fix (VoucherCommandService.java)
Open VoucherCommandService.java and locate the createBatchFromCacheAsync method.
**Find this exact line (around line 283 in your backend file):**
```java
voucherPostingRepository.save(posting);
workflowEngine.logWorkflowAction(batchId, vid, "Submitted", creatorId, commonRemarks, null);

```
**Replace it with this:**
```java
voucherPostingRepository.save(posting);

// FIX: Pass the actual scheduledTime to the workflow logger instead of null!
// This guarantees the Status screen receives the Creator's time in the history array.
String action = (scheduledTime != null) ? "Submitted (Scheduled)" : "Submitted";
workflowEngine.logWorkflowAction(batchId, vid, action, creatorId, commonRemarks, scheduledTime);

```
### Why this fixes the Status Screen automatically:
If you look at your frontend code for the Status screen (VoucherPostingStatus.jsx), you already have this logic built perfectly:
```javascript
// From VoucherPostingStatus.jsx
{history.SCHEDULED_TIME ? (
  <Chip
    label={isOverrideAction ? `Schedule Overridden to: ${formatDateTime(history.SCHEDULED_TIME)}` : `Scheduled for: ${formatDateTime(history.SCHEDULED_TIME)}`}
    // ...
  />
) : (
  (isSubmission || isFinalApproval) && !isSystem && (
    <Chip label="Immediate Execution" />
  )
)}

```
Because your React code for the Status screen correctly trusts history.SCHEDULED_TIME, the moment you apply that Java backend fix, the Status screen will instantly stop saying "Immediate Execution" and will output **"Scheduled for: [Creator's Time]"** on the first step, while keeping the master header updated with whatever the Final Approver decides.
